  "query_preview": "(This evening through Saturday)...\nIssued at 153 PM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows a a weak, unorganized trough \nof low pressure stretching from OH, across Central Indiana to \nIllinois. Strong High pressure was found over Ontario and Quebec. \nAloft, water vapor shows strong ridging in place over the plains, \nwith NW flow spilling across the Great Lakes and into Indiana. Water \nVapor also showed strong subsidence across the Great Lakes spilling \nsouth toward Indiana and Ohio. Dew points across Central Indiana \nwere recovering to around 70.\n\nTonight...\n\nDiurnal heating this afternoon along with the weak surface trough in \nplace across the area will lead to Isolated thunderstorm development \nlate this afternoon and this evening. CAMS suggest isolated \nconvection, but all of this wanes after 03Z as heating is lost and \nmore subsidence arrives. The main player through the overnight \nperiod will be the arrival of the high pressure system from the \nnortheast. The strong subsidence aloft will allow the high to push \nsouth. The leading edge of this high will act as a pseudo cold \nfront, pushing through trough further south. As the high arrives, \nwinds are expected to become northerly, allowing lower dewpoints and \ncooler temperatures to arrive. Forecast soundings show a dry column \novernight. Thus summing this all up, chances for showers and storms \nthis evening, becoming mostly clear and cooler overnight.\n\nSunday and Monday...\n\nThe upper pattern will remain relatively the same, as strong ridging \nto the west is expected to slowly push east toward Indiana. This set \nup will allow any forcing dynamics to be pushed well to the north \nacross the Great Lakes and Indiana will be protected. Meanwhile \nwithin the lower levels the cooler, Canadian high pressure system \nwill sag south across the Great Lakes. This will allow for cooler \nand dry NE flow to spill across Central Indiana on Sunday and \nMonday. Forecast soundings each day show unreachable convective \ntemperatures, a dry column and a strong mid level inversion in \nplace. Thus, mostly sunny days and mostly clear nights will be \nexpected, along with cooler highs in the mid 80s and less humidity.\n\nTuesday through Saturday...\n\nA well defined new pattern aloft is suggested to take hold during \nthis period. \n\nThe prominent and strong upper ridge that have been residing in the \nregion will finally break down on Tuesday Night into Wednesday. This \nshould allow for another day of heat on Tuesday before rain and \nstorm chances return on Tuesday Night into Wednesday.\n\nOn Wednesday through Saturday, Pacific NW flow takes over across the \nnorthern plains, streaming to Indiana. A series of upper level short \nwaves within that flow look to pass across Indiana during this time. \nUpper forcing with these waves look to pass at least once per day. \nWithin the lower levels, best chances for rain look to be on \nWednesday as a cold front passes, followed by Thursday Night through \nSaturday as a warm front arrives and Indiana looks to find itself \nwithin the warm sector of an approaching front. Temperatures look to \nbe in the 80s, above normal for much of the week ahead. Tuesday will \nlikely be the warmest day with highs around 90.",
